{{!-- =====================================================
     DETAILED PROPOSAL SCHEDULE
===================================================== --}}

<main class="sasha-practice-page">

  <div class="sasha-container">

    {{!-- =================================================
         INTRODUCTION
    ================================================== --}}

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-hero">

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-eyebrow">
        PROPOSAL MANAGEMENT
      </p>

      <h1>
        Detailed Proposal Schedule
      </h1>

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-intro">
        Build the proposal backwards from final production so every
        contribution, review, approval, and handoff happens before it
        becomes an emergency.
      </p>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         QUICK FACTS
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="schedule-quick-facts"
    >

      <h2 id="schedule-quick-facts">
        At a Glance
      </h2>

      <div class="sasha-practice-fact-grid">

        <article class="sasha-practice-fact-card">

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-label">
            TIME
          </p>

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-value">
            20–45 minutes
          </p>

          <p>
            A straightforward proposal may take only a few minutes to
            schedule. A large or multidisciplinary pursuit may require a
            more detailed planning session.
          </p>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-fact-card">

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-label">
            BEST FOR
          </p>

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-value">
            Usual and Full Effort
          </p>

          <p>
            A detailed schedule is especially valuable when several
            contributors, reviews, graphics, approvals, or production
            steps must be coordinated.
          </p>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-fact-card">

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-label">
            OUTPUT
          </p>

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-value">
            A working proposal plan
          </p>

          <p>
            Everyone knows what they are producing, when it is due,
            who owns it, and what work depends on it.
          </p>

        </article>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         WHO PARTICIPATES
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="schedule-participants"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <div>

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            WHO PARTICIPATES
          </p>

          <h2 id="schedule-participants">
            Build the schedule with the people who own the deadlines.
          </h2>

        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <p>
          The proposal manager usually builds and maintains the schedule,
          but contributor deadlines should not be invented in isolation.
          People who own important pieces of work should understand and
          commit to the dates that affect them.
        </p>


        <div class="sasha-practice-role-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">
            <strong>
              Proposal Manager
            </strong>

            <span>
              Builds the schedule, protects dependencies, tracks progress,
              and adjusts dates when circumstances change.
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">
            <strong>
              Pursuit Leader
            </strong>

            <span>
              Confirms priorities, important milestones, and senior
              decision points.
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">
            <strong>
              Technical Contributors
            </strong>

            <span>
              Confirm realistic delivery dates for writing, technical
              input, graphics, and reviews.
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">
            <strong>
              Reviewers
            </strong>

            <span>
              Confirm availability early enough that review periods are
              protected rather than squeezed into the final hours.
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">
            <strong>
              Pricing and Approvals
            </strong>

            <span>
              Confirm when fee information, signatures, commercial review,
              and approvals must occur.
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">
            <strong>
              Production Support
            </strong>

            <span>
              Identify time required for layout, final assembly, PDF
              generation, upload, printing, or delivery.
            </span>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         STEPS
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="schedule-steps"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <div>

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            THE PROCESS
          </p>

          <h2 id="schedule-steps">
            Build it backwards
          </h2>

        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-step-list">


        {{!-- STEP 1 --}}

        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            01
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Start with final production
            </h3>

            <p>
              Do not begin with the client deadline and simply work
              forward. Establish when the proposal team wants the final
              document complete and ready for submission.
            </p>

            <p class="sasha-practice-step-note">
              The client deadline is the hard stop. Your internal
              production deadline should come earlier.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        {{!-- STEP 2 --}}

        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            02
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Protect final quality-control time
            </h3>

            <p>
              Schedule enough time before final production for the
              activities that are often sacrificed when the proposal
              runs late.
            </p>

            <ul>
              <li>
                Proofreading
              </li>

              <li>
                Compliance checking
              </li>

              <li>
                Signatures and approvals
              </li>

              <li>
                PDF generation and document assembly
              </li>

              <li>
                Portal upload or delivery
              </li>

              <li>
                Time to resolve submission problems
              </li>
            </ul>

          </div>

        </article>


        {{!-- STEP 3 --}}

        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            03
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Schedule reviews before writing deadlines
            </h3>

            <p>
              Place major reviews on the schedule early enough that the
              team can actually respond to the findings.
            </p>

            <p>
              If a Red Team needs a substantially complete draft,
              contributor deadlines must occur early enough to assemble
              that draft first.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        {{!-- STEP 4 --}}

        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            04
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Add contributor deadlines
            </h3>

            <p>
              Work backward from each review, writing, graphics, pricing,
              and production milestone.
            </p>

            <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

              <strong>
                For every important contribution, define four things:
              </strong>

              <span>
                Who is responsible? What are they producing? When is it
                due? Who needs it next?
              </span>

            </div>

          </div>

        </article>


        {{!-- STEP 5 --}}

        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            05
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Identify the dependencies
            </h3>

            <p>
              A proposal schedule is not a collection of independent
              dates. One person's work often becomes someone else's
              starting point.
            </p>

            <ul>
              <li>
                Project examples may be needed before writing begins.
              </li>

              <li>
                Resumes may depend on final team selection.
              </li>

              <li>
                Graphics may depend on approved methodology content.
              </li>

              <li>
                Pricing may depend on the final work breakdown structure.
              </li>

              <li>
                Reviewers need a stable draft before they can review it.
              </li>
            </ul>

          </div>

        </article>


        {{!-- STEP 6 --}}

        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            06
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Confirm the dates with the team
            </h3>

            <p>
              Ask contributors to confirm that their assigned deadlines
              are realistic before the schedule becomes the working plan.
            </p>

            <p>
              If a deadline cannot be met, adjust the sequence while there
              is still time rather than discovering the conflict later.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         VALUE
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="schedule-value"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-value-panel">

        <div class="sasha-practice-value-heading">

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            WHY IT MATTERS
          </p>

          <h2 id="schedule-value">
            A schedule protects the work that makes the proposal better.
          </h2>

        </div>


        <div class="sasha-practice-value-copy">

          <p>
            Most proposal problems do not begin on submission day.
            They begin earlier, when one late contribution quietly
            compresses every activity that follows it.
          </p>

          <p>
            A detailed schedule makes those dependencies visible.
            It protects time for strategy, writing, reviews, graphics,
            proofreading, compliance, and final production instead of
            allowing all of them to compete for the same final hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also turns the proposal into managed work. People know
            what they own, what other people are waiting for, and what
            happens if a deadline slips.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         WHAT TO INCLUDE
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="schedule-ready"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
          WHAT BELONGS ON THE SCHEDULE
        </p>

        <h2 id="schedule-ready">
          Include the milestones that control the proposal.
        </h2>


        <div class="sasha-practice-check-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">
              ✓
            </span>

            <span>
              Proposal kickoff
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">
              ✓
            </span>

            <span>
              Win strategy or planning sessions
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">
              ✓
            </span>

            <span>
              Contributor writing deadlines
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">
              ✓
            </span>

            <span>
              Project and resume information deadlines
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">
              ✓
            </span>

            <span>
              Graphics and layout milestones
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">
              ✓
            </span>

            <span>
              Color Team or other review dates
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">
              ✓
            </span>

            <span>
              Pricing and approval milestones
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">
              ✓
            </span>

            <span>
              Proofreading and compliance review
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">
              ✓
            </span>

            <span>
              Final production
            </span>
          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">
              ✓
            </span>

            <span>
              Submission or upload
            </span>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         DECISION
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section
             sasha-practice-page-decision"
      aria-labelledby="schedule-decision"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-decision-panel">

        <div>

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            ADD TO THIS PURSUIT?
          </p>

          <h2 id="schedule-decision">
            Would you like to use a Detailed Proposal Schedule?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Later, Sasha will use this choice to build the appropriate
            planning milestones into the proposal schedule.
          </p>

        </div>


        <fieldset class="sasha-practice-page-options">

          <legend class="visually-hidden">
            Use Detailed Proposal Schedule
          </legend>


          <label>

            <input
              type="radio"
              name="useDetailedProposalSchedule"
              value="yes"
            >

            <span>
              Yes
            </span>

          </label>


          <label>

            <input
              type="radio"
              name="useDetailedProposalSchedule"
              value="no"
            >

            <span>
              No
            </span>

          </label>

        </fieldset>

      </div>

    </section>

  </div>

</main>